00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:05.110 align:start position:0%
the migrants are attempting the journey  in unseaworthy boats  every day if the weather is good  most are rescued at sea pulled to safety  by the emergency services  or plucked from the dangerous tides by  the border force  this group from africa and the middle  east picked up early  amongst them women and very young  children  the crossing can take up to 10 hours  navigating the busiest shipping lane in  the world  with just hope and prayer these people  bedraggled and tired arrived further  down the coastline  trudging up the beach carrying what few  belongings they could take with them  what about you where are you from sir  they traveled thousands of miles to get  here  from asia africa and the middle east  this is now a familiar scene on the kent  coastline  this group arrived in the last half an  hour they were rescued in the channel by  the lifeboat service  they've been given food and water  and they're just having their names and  their details their country where they  came from  taken now by immigration officials  policing the channel is becoming  increasingly difficult  and a political nightmare for the  government it's promising to fix what is  described  as the broken asylum system but making  the crossing a criminal offence  will do nothing to stop people  attempting the dangerous journey  according to human rights groups the  only way to claim asylum in this country  is to physically get here  so we have to find a way that people can  do it without risking their lives in  dinghies  one way would be humanitarian visas  another way would be increasing the  numbers that come by resettlement routes  but i don't think the government wants  to do either of those things  they like people to feel that they're  being overrun and that is just  isn't the case the home office says  an additional 55 million has been handed  to the french to clamp down  but with hundreds of miles of beaches  and the criminal gangs becoming more  sophisticated  it is a game of cat and mouse with the  authorities on this side of the channel  warning the tide of migration  won't stop alex rossi sky news  kent
 

